# api/

`api/openapi.yaml` lives here: the authoritative transport contract for this
application's JSON API. It is hand-edited. Both the Go server bindings under
`internal/platform/httpapi/openapigen/` and the frontend TypeScript models at
`frontend/src/lib/api/schema.d.ts` are generated from it.

Edit `openapi.yaml`, then run `make api-generate`. The pinned oapi-codegen
tool writes strict chi models and server bindings to
`internal/platform/httpapi/openapigen/openapi.gen.go`. `make api-check`
performs the same generation into a temporary file and fails on a byte
difference without modifying the checked-in output; it is part of `make all`.

The generation config vendors the pinned strict-server templates inline. They
route declared JSON bodies through `internal/platform/httpapi/httpjson`: one
value, no unknown or case-insensitively duplicated fields, an exact declared
media type, at most 100 container levels, and a 1 MiB request limit. Optional
zero-byte multi-content bodies remain absent, while nonempty bodies require a
declared type. Generated buffered JSON responses and
the package's `Write` helper share a 1 MiB pre-commit response limit. Keep the
inline templates aligned with the pinned oapi-codegen defaults during an
upgrade; do not replace them with remote template URLs.

`components.schemas.Problem` is the one RFC 9457 wire contract for server and
frontend models. It permits only the standard members plus `code`,
`request_id`, and `correlation_id`; add any future extension here explicitly.
The application-owned `internal/platform/httpapi/problem` package supplies
validated catalog definitions and the bounded `application/problem+json`
writer. Generated request/response failures, API `404`/`405`, and API recovery
all use it without serializing or logging arbitrary cause values. Recovery
stages at most 1 MiB until normal return; explicit streaming remains
passthrough, and a post-flush panic aborts rather than appending Problem JSON.

Run `make api-types-generate` for the checked-in frontend model and
`make api-types-check` for its non-mutating openapi-typescript `--check` gate.
The application-owned `frontend/src/lib/api/client.ts` wraps those path types
with fixed same-origin credentials, cancellation, and stable error mapping.

Paths in the document are relative to the `/api/v1` server and router
boundary. For example, `/widgets` becomes `/api/v1/widgets`; do not repeat
the version prefix in every OpenAPI path. The API index at `GET /api/v1/`
returns the direct version resource and is not a health endpoint.

Return a single resource as the body itself. Define every collection as a
concrete closed `{items,page}` schema: `items` is required and encodes empty as
`[]`, while `page` is a required non-null object. `APIRootCollection` is the
generated contract fixture, not a public list endpoint. Domain APIs should
define schemas and constructors such as `InvoiceCollection`; each constructor
must normalize a nil Go slice to `[]` before writing. Do not add a generic
runtime envelope or `data`, `result`, and `meta` wrappers.

An operational list pages by cursor. Give it a `page` of `CursorPage` and
reference the shared `CursorLimit`, `CursorAfter`, and `CursorBefore` parameter
components so every collection names the same three parameters.
`next_cursor` and `prev_cursor` are absent, never null, when there is no page
in that direction. `APIRootCursorCollection` is the fixture.

A report — where a page number is part of the answer — uses the separate
`ReportPage` contract with the `ReportPageNumber` and `ReportPageSize`
parameters and `APIRootReportCollection` as its fixture. It is not a mode of
cursor paging: the parameters differ, and a request combining them is refused.
`issueReportPage` echoes the requested page and size back rather than
recomputing them, and `pagination.NewReportLimits` sets the depth ceiling past
which a page is refused as `report_too_deep`. Prefer cursors for anything a
user scrolls; reach for a report only when the page number itself matters.

In `api.go`, `cursorBinding` fingerprints the query a cursor belongs to —
every parameter except `limit`, `after`, and `before` — and `issueCursorPage`
mints one authenticated cursor per direction that has a further page. Query one
row more than the requested limit to learn `has_more`, sort by a unique key,
and put any filter that is not in the query string (a path parameter, the
caller's tenant) into the resource argument. Pagination failures are `400`
with `invalid_pagination` or `cursor_expired`; `paginationProblem` does that
mapping. A cursor is signed with `CURSOR_SIGNING_KEY` and expires after
`CURSOR_TTL`.

The API and document routers are sibling security boundaries with independent
policies. Application middleware may be added only through the documented
`httpapi.MiddlewareSlots`, inside the fixed security, request identity/logging,
recovery, and metrics core. Keep `router_test.go` current if the application
deliberately changes that contract.

Implement generated operations on the application-owned `httpapi.Server` in
`api.go`. Its compile-time `StrictServerInterface` assertion makes a changed
contract fail the build until every operation has an implementation. Never
edit `openapigen/`; it retains oapi-codegen's standard generated-code header
and is replaced as a complete tool-owned output. The application adapter
injects the `httpjson` decoder and size checker through generated strict-server
options, so generated code never imports application code or creates an import
cycle.
